                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the root cause and the cost of quality`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Open the line-level defect data. Your Pareto shows that <strong>~60% of defects come from the aging enamel-application process itself</strong> — a process limitation that statistical control can tighten but not fix. The rest is variation. Decide whether this is a process problem calling for <strong>process innovation</strong> or an execution problem calling for continuous improvement alone, because that diagnosis operationalizes your project choice. Then reframe the spend: cost of quality is <strong>R$64,8 mi/year</strong> — scrap, rework, returns, warranty — more than two-thirds of EBITDA. Closing the gap to best practice recovers <em>~R$28,8 mi/year</em>. Is this an expense, or an investment against a bleed?`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the innovation project inside R$22 mi`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Three candidates, one envelope. <strong>Process A</strong> (R$19 mi, 6-month install): the electrostatic enamel + in-line vision system gets defects to ~1.6% but removes ~180,000 units of capacity during install. <strong>Product B</strong> (R$14 mi): a premium nano-coating extension with real revenue upside that <em>raises near-term defect risk on the very lines CasaForte is watching</em>. <strong>Continuous Improvement C</strong> (R$4,5 mi): fast, low-downtime DMAIC + SPC that plateaus around 2.8% — it cannot fix the process root cause. You cannot do all three. Concentrate the money where the Pareto points, and commit enough to reach critical mass without breaching R$22 mi.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Set quality metrics and allocate resources`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Decide what you manage to. A <strong>single defect-rate target invites gaming</strong> — you can hit 1.9% with 100% manual re-inspection that pushes cycle time up 22% and cost of quality to 11%. A <strong>balanced scorecard</strong> (defect rate, first-pass yield, cycle time, cost of quality) aligns the floor. Then allocate engineering and floor-time effort across the lines: concentrate on the watched CasaForte lines <em>without neglecting the others</em>, and reserve effort for the SPC control phase that locks gains in. Metrics drive behavior — choose the set that aligns the system, not the number that flatters the regulator.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Protect delivery while improving quality`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The install is under way and the plant is near its ceiling. <strong>Sequence the rollout</strong>: phase it line-by-line so three lines keep feeding CasaForte, or take all the lines down at once and finish fast. <strong>Bridge the ~180,000 lost units</strong> with overtime and temporary outsourcing of low-complexity volume, or renegotiate a phased target. Then the <em>mid-project trial underperforms</em> — diagnose and adjust, push the deadline anyway, or fall back to the improvement program. Deliver the three-minute board pitch: defect rate, cost of quality, cycle time and pipeline value, projected and defended.`,

