                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_process:       `<strong>That matches the Pareto.</strong> If ~60% of defects come from the enamel-application process itself, SPC can tighten the variation but it cannot fix the process — that is a case for process innovation. You have read the data, not your wish list.`,
                    coach_r1_execution:     `<strong>Careful — that under-reads the Pareto.</strong> An execution-only diagnosis sends you to continuous improvement alone, which plateaus around 2.8% because the dominant driver is the aging process itself. When is tightening variation not enough, and you need to replace the process?`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>Blaming the operators is the comfortable answer.</strong> When 60% of defects trace to one aging process, that is a system limitation, not careless people. Retraining the floor will not move a process-bound defect rate.`,
                    coach_r1_random:        `<strong>The Pareto is not flat.</strong> Defects concentrate — ~60% on the enamel-application process. Calling it random noise means you will spread effort everywhere and fix nothing. Read where the cost concentrates.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You matched the intervention to a process root cause AND reframed quality as a R$64,8 mi bleed, not a discretionary expense. That is exactly the lens that wins the board conversation and points the budget at the right project.`,
                    coach_r1_expense:       `<strong>You read the root cause but framed the money as a cost.</strong> The board balks at R$19 mi until you set it against R$64,8 mi of annual failure cost. Reframe the spend as an investment that recovers ~R$28,8 mi/year, or the right project gets cut.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring cost of quality loses the argument.</strong> If you pitch only the CasaForte deadline, the board sees a R$19 mi expense, not a recovery against a R$64,8 mi bleed. Cost of quality is your strongest case — use it.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>A thin diagnosis poisons every later decision.</strong> Without a real Pareto and cost-of-quality breakdown you are guessing which project to fund. Build the root-cause analysis before you spend a real.`,
                    coach_r2_apc:           `<strong>This concentrates the money where the Pareto points.</strong> Process A fixes the aging-process root cause to ~1.6%, and continuous improvement with SPC locks the gains in. You funded it to critical mass inside the envelope — now the bind is the install downtime.`,
                    coach_r2_aonly:         `<strong>Process A will get you to ~1.6% — excellent.</strong> But without the SPC control phase the gains can backslide, and you have left R$3 mi of envelope unused. Pairing A with continuous improvement locks in what the install achieves.`,
                    coach_r2_bpc:           `<strong>You launched the nano-coating extension for the revenue upside.</strong> But it added process complexity and your near-term defect rate ticked <em>up</em> on the very lines CasaForte is watching. Was new revenue worth the R$86,5 mi you were trying to protect? Stabilize quality before you add complexity.`,
                    coach_r2_conly:         `<strong>DMAIC and SPC are smart and low-downtime — but bounded.</strong> Your Pareto shows 60% of defects come from the aging process itself, which SPC can tighten but not fix. You will plateau around 2.8%, short of the 2.0% target. When is continuous improvement not enough?`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>You funded a little of everything.</strong> Half a process line, a pilot coating cell, a partial Six Sigma rollout — none reaches critical mass and the defect rate barely moves. With one binding deadline, where should the money have concentrated?`,
                    coach_r2_starved:       `<strong>You picked the right project but starved it.</strong> Under-funding the process install below critical mass means the install stalls and the defect gains never fully land. Commit enough of the R$22 mi to actually finish it.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A balanced scorecard aligns the floor.</strong> Managing defect rate alongside first-pass yield, cycle time and cost of quality stops the team from buying the headline number at the business's expense. That is metrics driving the right behavior.`,
                    coach_r3_defectonly:    `<strong>A single defect-rate target invites gaming.</strong> You can hit 1.9% by inflating cycle time and cost of quality — passing the test CasaForte set and failing the business. What should a balanced scorecard have included?`,
                    coach_r3_output:        `<strong>Managing only to throughput keeps the lines running — and the defects flowing.</strong> Units shipped is the wrong North Star when a customer ultimatum is about defects. You will protect output and miss the 2.0% target.`,
                    coach_r3_allocgood:     `<strong>This is a balanced allocation.</strong> You concentrated effort on the watched CasaForte lines without neglecting the others, and reserved real effort for the SPC control phase. The gains will hold instead of backsliding.`,
                    coach_r3_overconcentrate: `<strong>You fixed CasaForte and neglected the rest.</strong> Pouring almost all the effort onto the watched lines lifts their numbers but lets the other lines drift — and the defect reputation can spread to the two other large accounts (R$120 mi at risk).`,
                    coach_r3_nocontrol:     `<strong>No control phase means the gains backslide.</strong> Without SPC effort reserved to hold the new process, the improvement you bought erodes the moment attention moves. The control phase is what locks in the win.`,
                    coach_r3_reinspect:     `<strong>You hit the number with 100% manual re-inspection.</strong> But cycle time blew out and cost of quality climbed past 11% of revenue. You passed the test CasaForte set and failed the business — that is exactly the single-metric trap.`,
                    coach_r4_phased:        `<strong>Phasing the install respects the capacity bind.</strong> Keeping three lines feeding CasaForte while one is down protects peak-season volume and still moves the defect rate. You solved the quality problem without creating a delivery problem.`,
                    coach_r4_alllines:      `<strong>Taking all the lines down at once finishes fast — and starves CasaForte.</strong> The 6-month install removes ~180,000 units right through the high season, and you cannot supply the peak orders. You solved quality by creating a delivery crisis.`,
                    coach_r4_delay:         `<strong>Delaying the install protects delivery but misses the clock.</strong> Push the process project past peak season and the defect rate stays above 2.0% through the two quarters — CasaForte triggers the dual-source clause. The bind was to do both, not choose one.`,
                    coach_r4_bridgegood:    `<strong>Overtime plus temporary outsourcing bridges the gap.</strong> Outsourcing low-complexity volume and running extra shifts keeps CasaForte's peak orders supplied while the line is down. That is sequencing the constraint, not ignoring it.`,
                    coach_r4_bridgenothing: `<strong>Absorbing the shortfall is hoping, not planning.</strong> ~180,000 units of lost capacity during peak season means missed CasaForte orders — the exact volume you are trying to protect. Bridge it with overtime and outsourcing.`,
                    coach_r4_renegotiate:   `<strong>A phased target buys time but concedes ground.</strong> Renegotiating with CasaForte protects the relationship, yet without bridging the lost volume you are slower to the 2.0% target and exposed if they lose patience. Pair it with capacity cover.`,
                    coach_r4_push:          `<strong>Pushing a failed trial into full rollout is how you scale a defect.</strong> The trial underperformed for a reason; rolling it out unadjusted raises the defect rate and the cost of quality. Diagnose and adjust before you commit the lines.`,
                    coach_r4_fallback:      `<strong>Falling back to continuous improvement abandons the root-cause fix.</strong> One disappointing trial is not a verdict on the process project — and SPC alone plateaus above 2.0%. Adjust the trial parameters rather than retreating to a bounded option.`,
                    coach_r4_strong:        `<strong>A board-ready recovery.</strong> You phased the install, bridged the lost volume, and adjusted the trial instead of pushing or retreating — defect rate on a credible path below 2.0% with CasaForte's peak orders protected. Quality and delivery, won together.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
