                    insight_won:            `Quality and delivery won together: the defect rate is on a credible path below 2.0%, cost of quality is recovering toward best practice, and CasaForte's peak volume stayed protected. You matched the process innovation to the root cause and sequenced the install around the capacity ceiling.`,
                    insight_gamed:          `You hit the defect number but failed the business: manual re-inspection forced the headline down while cycle time and cost of quality blew out. You passed the test CasaForte set and broke the economics — the classic single-metric trap.`,
                    insight_delivery:       `You solved quality by creating a delivery problem. The defect rate met the target, but the install starved CasaForte's peak-season orders — the exact volume the project was meant to protect. The bind was to do both.`,
                    insight_thin:           `You met the target, but on thin foundations — without a control phase to hold the gains or a balanced scorecard, the improvement is fragile and the cost of quality has not recovered.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short of the <2.0% target. CasaForte can now dual-source 50% of its volume (R$86,5 mi), and the defect reputation can spread to two other accounts (a further R$120 mi). Re-examine whether your project matched the process root cause.`,
                    insight_breach:         `You committed more than the R$22 mi board envelope — the financing case the board approved no longer holds, and the over-spend undermines the cost-of-quality argument you needed to win.`,
                    insight_coqup:          `Cost of quality rose rather than fell — usually the signature of forcing the defect number with re-inspection or added complexity instead of fixing the process. Quality spend should recover margin, not consume more of it.`,
                    insight_lowpipe:        `The innovation pipeline stayed thin. Choosing the bounded improvement program or spreading the budget left little revenue or capability upside — quality may be steadier, but the Forum's innovation logic was underused.`,
                    insight_bestinclass:    `You reached the best-in-class defect level of ~1.5% — exceptional, but only meaningful if delivery held and cost of quality recovered alongside it.`,
                    insight_balanced:       `This is balanced operations leadership: defect rate, cost of quality, delivery and pipeline all moving the right way at once. Innovation and quality were managed as one competitive-operations system, not as competing agendas.`,

                    hint_breach:            `You have breached the R$22 mi envelope. The board approved one major project plus improvement, not all three — trim the commitment back inside the cap.`,
                    hint_gamed:             `You are forcing the defect number with manual re-inspection — cost of quality and cycle time are paying for it. Fix the process and use a balanced scorecard instead of inspecting defects out.`,
                    hint_delivery:          `CasaForte delivery is dangerously low. Any project that takes a line down competes with the volume you are protecting — phase the install and bridge the lost units with overtime or outsourcing.`,
                    hint_behind:            `You are behind the <2.0% target with little time left. Check that your project actually addresses the process root cause — continuous improvement alone plateaus around 2.8%.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — defect rate below target, delivery protected, cost of quality recovering. Lock the gains in with the SPC control phase and a balanced scorecard so they hold.`,

